A||||4||88formula|| I degreed my camshaft on my 3.4 for GM 12363220 camshaft. ||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||06-13-2001||09:43 PM||||Put the Degree wheel on my GM 12363220 camshaft and checked the phasing of the camshaft. It was three degrees out so I had to move the key to the –3 retard notch to correct this. I then checked the lobe ceterline and it was 107. The lobe separation angle was 111.5. The valve timing events I got @ .050” where intake opens @ -5 and closes @ 29. Exhaust opens @ 44 and closes @ -8. These are about the same specifications as the Crane PowerMax H-260-2 camshaft. The Crane PowerMax timing events are intake opens @ -5 and closes @ 29. Exhaust opens @ 45 and closes @ -9. The lobe center is 107 and the lobe separtion angle is 112.  <P>This really sucks because GM charges twice the money for the same camshaft as the Crane PowerMax H-260-2 camshaft. Instead of 210 horsepower my engine will only make 203 horsepower @ 5000 rpm. It will make 237-ft. lbs. of torque from 3500 rpm to 4000 rpm. Torque is up but horsepower is down. I’ve got to figure a way to retard the cam three more degrees to move the horsepower up in the power band. If I can do that than it will make 206 horsepower @ 5500 rpm and 235-ft. lbs. of torque @ 4000 rpm, which would be better for acceleration. <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Shaun41178||06-14-2001||03:57 PM||||Thanks for the numbers Formula.  <P>You are going to try and get the cam retarded more then it can already go just for 3 hp?  Are there more retard notches or is it as far as it will go?  Seems like a bit of work for just 3 hp.  You pick up 3 hp but you also lose a few lb-ft of torque so where is the benefit?<P>How much does GM charge for that cam?  I was able to get the 059 cam for $114 with the lifters.<P>Thanks again for the cam numbers<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Preferred Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steven||06-14-2001||05:01 PM||||Well, now that the cam is in, did you plan on cc the block with the piston at TDC? If you do, could you please pass that info along, I was wondering how my engine will stand up compared to yours. LMK. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||06-14-2001||06:30 PM||||Shaun I'm trying to phase the cam so that I get the best performance out of it. Three horsepower is just the peak number. At 6000 rpm there is an 8 horsepower difference. With the camshaft retarded I don't lose more than 3-ft. lbs. of torque in the rpm band over the 0-setting. Last I checked the GM 12363220 was about $100 and the lifters you had to buy separately for about $90. I have the timing sprocket set to the –3 mark because the cam phasing was out +3, which makes the cam 0. <P>Using the Drag program this translates into a 0-60 mph time of 5.61 seconds and the quarter mile in 13.89 @ 97.5 mph if the camshaft is set to 0. If it’s three degrees retarded I get a 0-60 mph in 5.6 seconds and a quarter mile in 13.87 seconds @ 97.5 mph, just picky I guess. <P>Head gasket volume is 6.87 cc<BR>Piston dish volume is 12 cc<BR>Deck volume is 13.71 cc because the piston is .010” below the deck @ TDC<BR>Average head chamber volume is 50 cc<BR>Total combustion volume is 70.58 cc<BR>Swept cylinder volume is 567.55 cc<BR>Bore is 3.650<BR>Stroke is 3.310<BR>CID is 207.8 (3.4-liter)<BR>Compression ratio is 9.04<P>  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bill H||06-14-2001||06:36 PM||||Hey 88 you would not happen to know the torq specs for rod and main bearing on a 95 3.4 do you?||||reg||1
